---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = "Whale Watching Season in Hawaii: Your Complete Guide";
const description = "Humpback whales visit Hawaii from December through April. Learn the best spots, tours, and tips for whale watching on the Big Island near Hilo.";
const date = "2025-11-20";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="blog-post">
    <header class="post-header">
      <nav class="breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/blog">Blog</a> / Whale Watching
      </nav>
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <div class="post-meta">
        <time datetime={date}>November 20, 2025</time>
        <span class="category">Nature</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="post-content">
      <p class="lead">
        Every winter, thousands of humpback whales migrate from Alaska to the warm Hawaiian
        waters to breed, give birth, and nurse their calves. From December through April,
        the Big Island becomes one of the best places on Earth to witness these magnificent
        creatures—and you might even spot them from your lanai at Island Goodes!
      </p>

      <h2>When to Visit for Whale Watching</h2>
      <p>
        While whales begin arriving in November and some linger until May, the peak season is:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Best months:</strong> January through March</li>
        <li><strong>Peak activity:</strong> Mid-February to mid-March</li>
        <li><strong>Early/late season:</strong> December and April offer fewer crowds</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        During peak season, an estimated 10,000 humpback whales visit Hawaiian waters—about
        two-thirds of the entire North Pacific population!
      </p>

      <h2>Whale Watching from Island Goodes</h2>
      <p>
        One of the special benefits of staying with us is the ocean view from our property.
        Guests in the Hilo Bay Room and Orchid Room have spotted whales right from their
        private lanais! Look for:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Spouts:</strong> Tall, misty exhalations visible from miles away</li>
        <li><strong>Breaching:</strong> Whales leaping out of the water—spectacular!</li>
        <li><strong>Tail slaps:</strong> Whales raising their flukes above the surface</li>
        <li><strong>Pec slaps:</strong> Whales slapping their long pectoral fins</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We have binoculars available for guests—just ask!
      </p>

      <h2>Best Shore Viewing Spots</h2>

      <h3>Near Hilo (East Side)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Leleiwi Beach Park:</strong> Elevated viewing point with morning sun behind you</li>
        <li><strong>Richardson Beach:</strong> Rocky points offer good vantage points</li>
        <li><strong>Coconut Island:</strong> 360-degree views from Hilo Bay</li>
        <li><strong>Pepe'ekeo Scenic Drive:</strong> Pull-offs with ocean views</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Kohala Coast (West Side)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Puako:</strong> Calm waters where mothers and calves rest</li>
        <li><strong>Lapakahi State Historical Park:</strong> Elevated coastal views</li>
        <li><strong>Pololu Valley Lookout:</strong> Dramatic cliffside viewing</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Whale Watching Tours</h2>
      <p>
        For up-close encounters, consider a boat tour:
      </p>

      <h3>From Hilo</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Several operators run seasonal tours from Hilo Harbor</li>
        <li>Smaller boats mean more intimate experiences</li>
        <li>Morning tours typically have calmer seas</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>From Kona</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>More tour options and larger boats</li>
        <li>Calmer Kona waters can mean better viewing conditions</li>
        <li>Many tours guarantee sightings or offer free return trip</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Tour Tips</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Book in advance during peak season</li>
        <li>Take motion sickness medication if prone to seasickness</li>
        <li>Bring layers—it's cooler on the water</li>
        <li>Bring a camera with good zoom capability</li>
        <li>Listen to naturalist guides—they share fascinating facts</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Humpback Whale Facts</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Size:</strong> Adults reach 45-60 feet long, weighing 40 tons</li>
        <li><strong>Migration:</strong> They travel 3,000 miles from Alaska—one of the longest migrations of any mammal</li>
        <li><strong>Fasting:</strong> Whales don't eat while in Hawaii; they live off blubber reserves</li>
        <li><strong>Songs:</strong> Male humpbacks "sing" complex songs that can last 20 minutes</li>
        <li><strong>Calves:</strong> Newborns are 10-15 feet long and gain 100 pounds per day nursing</li>
        <li><strong>Protection:</strong> Humpbacks are protected under the Endangered Species Act</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Responsible Whale Watching</h2>
      <p>
        Hawaii has strict laws protecting whales:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Stay at least 100 yards from whales by boat or in water</li>
        <li>Don't chase, surround, or approach whales</li>
        <li>If whales approach you, put engine in neutral</li>
        <li>Never swim with or touch whales</li>
        <li>Report distressed whales to NOAA: 1-888-256-9840</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="cta-box">
        <h3>Book Your Whale Season Stay</h3>
        <p>Visit Island Goodes during whale season (December-April) and experience the magic of humpback whales—possibly from your own lanai!</p>
        <a href="https://v2.reservationkey.com/3655/reserve/c" class="btn btn-primary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Check Availability</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .blog-post {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 2rem 4rem;
  }

  .post-header {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 8rem 0 3rem;
  }

  .breadcrumb {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    color: var(--color-text-light);
  }

  .breadcrumb a {
    color: var(--color-primary);
  }

  .post-header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 2.5rem);
    color: var(--color-primary-dark);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .post-meta {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 1rem;
    color: var(--color-text-light);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .category {
    background: var(--color-secondary);
    color: #fff;
    padding: 0.2rem 0.75rem;
    border-radius: 20px;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
  }

  .post-content {
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .post-content .lead {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    color: var(--color-text-light);
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .post-content h2 {
    color: var(--color-primary-dark);
    margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem;
    font-size: 1.5rem;
  }

  .post-content h3 {
    color: var(--color-primary);
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0.75rem;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
  }

  .post-content p {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .post-content ul {
    margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem 1.5rem;
  }

  .post-content li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .cta-box {
    background: var(--color-bg-alt);
    padding: 2rem;
    border-radius: 8px;
    text-align: center;
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }

  .cta-box h3 {
    color: var(--color-primary-dark);
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .cta-box p {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .btn {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.875rem 2rem;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: all 0.3s;
  }

  .btn-primary {
    background: var(--color-secondary);
    color: #fff;
  }

  .btn-primary:hover {
    background: #c49943;
  }
</style>
